        "contenuto_esteso": "To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. Once seeking de-escalation, Saudi Arabia now sees ‘no coexistence’ with hardline Iran Middle East Issued on: 25/03/2026 - 16:28 Modified: 25/03/2026 - 16:32 Nadia Massih is pleased to welcome Natasha Lindstaedt, Author, Professor of Government at the University of Essex. The unfolding conflict reveals a profound shift in Gulf strategic thinking, Professor Lindstaedt argues, driven less by ideological ambition than by accumulated insecurity. What began as a strong preference for de-escalation has gradually given way to a more hard-edged assessment of regional realities. The persistence of attacks, particularly on critical infrastructure, has reinforced the perception that coexistence with a hardline Iranian regime may no longer be viable. US goals shift: 'At the beginning of the Iran war, the US and Israel were on the same page' 25/03/2026 Middle East US demands of 15-point peace plan likely 'unacceptable' for Iran 25/03/2026 Middle East Unclear 'how much of a negotiation is taking place' between US and Iran 25/03/2026 Middle East What do we know of the US's 15-point peace plan sent to Iran? 25/03/2026 Middle East Inside the Palestinian camp in Beirut where residents are staying put, for now 25/03/2026 Middle East In Lebanon, some schools choose to stay open despite the war 25/03/2026 Middle East US ramps up military presence in the Middle East amid reported talks 25/03/2026 Middle East US sends 15-point peace plan to Iran while deploying troops in the region 25/03/2026 Middle East 'The humanitarian crisis here is worsening by the minute': Israel continues to bomb Lebanon 25/03/2026 Middle East"
